#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1251).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "GraphLiteral,4255304429"
msgid "Species: Ignis leoforma\r\nTime: Unknown\r\nDiet: Carnivore\r\nTemperament: Aggressive\r\n\r\nWild\r\nIs Pyromane a cat that controls fire, or living fire that can make itself look like a cat? It does seem to have more in common with the Phoenix from that desert ARK than any lion. An author named Clarke said sufficiently advanced technology would be indistinguishable from magic… I’d say that whatever engineered this creature was plenty advanced.\r\n\r\n\r\nAn unruffled Pyromane will stay at a low burn and shrink its profile. But when provoked, that small cat can suddenly flare itself up into a lion-sized inferno. It seems to drink in any nearby fire, using that power to cauterize its own wounds and power up its arsenal of incendiary attacks.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nI've only seen one tame Pyromane so far. Its rider seemed unbothered by the beast’s flames, and beyond that immune to heat in general while mounted. She claimed it could even carry her safely across a lava field. That Survivor said she spent ages trying to win her cat over, before noticing it seemed more sluggish in the rain.\r\n\r\n\r\nIn its smaller form, her pet doubled as a shoulder-mounted, feline flamethrower. And back at their campsite, she showed me how her pet could fuel a forge and dry meat into jerky. I can definitely see why such an improbable but versatile creature might have been engineered, even if I don’t understand how.\r\n\r\n\r\n"
msgstr "Species: Ignis leoforma\r\nTime: Unknown\r\nDiet: Carnivore\r\nTemperament: Aggressive\r\n\r\nWild\r\nIs Pyromane a cat that controls fire, or living fire that can make itself look like a cat? It does seem to have more in common with the Phoenix from that desert ARK than any lion. An author named Clarke said sufficiently advanced technology would be indistinguishable from magic… I’d say that whatever engineered this creature was plenty advanced.\r\n\r\n\r\nAn unruffled Pyromane will stay at a low burn and shrink its profile. But when provoked, that small cat can suddenly flare itself up into a lion-sized inferno. It seems to drink in any nearby fire, using that power to cauterize its own wounds and power up its arsenal of incendiary attacks.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nI've only seen one tame Pyromane so far. Its rider seemed unbothered by the beast’s flames, and beyond that immune to heat in general while mounted. She claimed it could even carry her safely across a lava field. That Survivor said she spent ages trying to win her cat over, before noticing it seemed more sluggish in the rain.\r\n\r\n\r\nIn its smaller form, her pet doubled as a shoulder-mounted, feline flamethrower. And back at their campsite, she showed me how her pet could fuel a forge and dry meat into jerky. I can definitely see why such an improbable but versatile creature might have been engineered, even if I don’t understand how.\r\n\r\n\r\n"

#. Key:	1279054806
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1252).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1252).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "GraphLiteral,1279054806"
msgid "Species: Gigantoraptor nutritor\r\nTime: Late Cretaceous\r\nDiet: Omnivore\r\nTemperament: Territorial\r\n\r\nWild\r\nUnless you’re planning to raid its nest, you shouldn’t let this creature’s imposing size worry you. Gigantoraptor may be fiercely protective of its brood, but it’s much more of a caregiver than a fighter.\r\n\r\nDon’t get me wrong -- this theropod will use its sharp claws and surprising speed to defend its young when threatened. But Gigantoraptor has a soft heart for young ones -- it’ll even take in the orphaned babies of rival species.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nNow for the bad news: to my knowledge, no one’s been able to figure out how to tame an adult Gigantoraptor -- its unique disposition seems resistant to behavioral conditioning. However, Survivors bolder than me were able to sneak into nests to win the trust of their young, and they claim that reward is well worth the risk.\r\n\r\nOnce matured to adulthood, your tamed Gigantoraptor will happily look after any of your tribe’s young creatures, nurturing them and teaching them how to fight. It can even use its call to help you find orphaned baby animals out in the wild. If you bring those orphans home, then your Gigantoraptor will happily add them to its brood.\r\n\r\nGigantoraptor forms an inseparable bond with its young charges. Bring them into a fight together and they will defend each other, and your tribe, far more effectively than when apart."
msgstr "Species: Gigantoraptor nutritor\r\nTime: Late Cretaceous\r\nDiet: Omnivore\r\nTemperament: Territorial\r\n\r\nWild\r\nUnless you’re planning to raid its nest, you shouldn’t let this creature’s imposing size worry you. Gigantoraptor may be fiercely protective of its brood, but it’s much more of a caregiver than a fighter.\r\n\r\nDon’t get me wrong -- this theropod will use its sharp claws and surprising speed to defend its young when threatened. But Gigantoraptor has a soft heart for young ones -- it’ll even take in the orphaned babies of rival species.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nNow for the bad news: to my knowledge, no one’s been able to figure out how to tame an adult Gigantoraptor -- its unique disposition seems resistant to behavioral conditioning. However, Survivors bolder than me were able to sneak into nests to win the trust of their young, and they claim that reward is well worth the risk.\r\n\r\nOnce matured to adulthood, your tamed Gigantoraptor will happily look after any of your tribe’s young creatures, nurturing them and teaching them how to fight. It can even use its call to help you find orphaned baby animals out in the wild. If you bring those orphans home, then your Gigantoraptor will happily add them to its brood.\r\n\r\nGigantoraptor forms an inseparable bond with its young charges. Bring them into a fight together and they will defend each other, and your tribe, far more effectively than when apart."

#. Key:	1839555793
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1253).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1253).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "GraphLiteral,1839555793"
msgid "Common Name: Deinosuchus\r\nSpecies: Deinosuchus Sanguineus \r\nTime: Late Cretaceous \r\nDiet: Carnivore \r\nTemperament: Aggressive \r\n\r\nI believed Sarcosuchus was the largest crocodilian on The Island, until I encountered Deinosuchus. Massive and bulky, this specimen seems to actually be a mix of its' namesake, and a similar species known as Purussaurus. \r\n\r\nDeionosuchus sport huge and powerful jaws, wich they utilize to break the bones of their prey, and halt their escape. Attempting to escape while suffering the effects of their bite can lead to your demise. This coupled with their incredibly tough hide can make disposing of Deinosuchus a difficult and dangerous task.\r\n\r\nWhen tamed, Deinosuchus can make quick work of many creatures, both land and water. Most creatures seem to lack the intelligence to halt their movement when suffering the desctructive effects of a Deinosuchus bite, and this ultimately costs them their life. Any tribe in control of a  Deinosuchus packs a dangerous punch... or in this case... bite!"
msgstr "Common Name: Deinosuchus\r\nSpecies: Deinosuchus Sanguineus \r\nTime: Late Cretaceous \r\nDiet: Carnivore \r\nTemperament: Aggressive \r\n\r\nI believed Sarcosuchus was the largest crocodilian on The Island, until I encountered Deinosuchus. Massive and bulky, this specimen seems to actually be a mix of its' namesake, and a similar species known as Purussaurus. \r\n\r\nDeionosuchus sport huge and powerful jaws, wich they utilize to break the bones of their prey, and halt their escape. Attempting to escape while suffering the effects of their bite can lead to your demise. This coupled with their incredibly tough hide can make disposing of Deinosuchus a difficult and dangerous task.\r\n\r\nWhen tamed, Deinosuchus can make quick work of many creatures, both land and water. Most creatures seem to lack the intelligence to halt their movement when suffering the desctructive effects of a Deinosuchus bite, and this ultimately costs them their life. Any tribe in control of a  Deinosuchus packs a dangerous punch... or in this case... bite!"

#. Key:	1604040513
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1254).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1254).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "GraphLiteral,1604040513"
msgid "Species: Yi Ling\r\nTime: Late Jurassic\r\nDiet: Omnivore\r\nTemperament: Aloof\r\n\r\nWild\r\nThis feathered fella is so much bigger than the Yi species I knew in my day! Despite its aberrant size, Yi ling is still somehow light enough to cling to walls and it can even glide short distances. It’s light on its feet as well, able to dodge attacks on the run.\r\nI’m glad Yi seems to prefer prey smaller than itself, because it can eject a spray of barbed feathers at an unhappy target before darting in to rip them back out. Its unique firepower may expose Yi in the short term, but its protective plumage regrows fast.\r\n\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nA tamed Yi makes for an excellent -- if precarious -- hunting mount. Some tribes use them as caravan scouts, others to torment enemy forces.\r\nI’ve seen Yi scarf up the weird mushrooms, congealed gas balls, and nameless creatures scurrying around down here. Who knows what effect a diet like that would have on its feathery weaponry? Watch what your Yi eats, and you just might learn to sharpen its fighting skills!"
msgstr "Species: Yi Ling\r\nTime: Late Jurassic\r\nDiet: Omnivore\r\nTemperament: Aloof\r\n\r\nWild\r\nThis feathered fella is so much bigger than the Yi species I knew in my day! Despite its aberrant size, Yi ling is still somehow light enough to cling to walls and it can even glide short distances. It’s light on its feet as well, able to dodge attacks on the run.\r\nI’m glad Yi seems to prefer prey smaller than itself, because it can eject a spray of barbed feathers at an unhappy target before darting in to rip them back out. Its unique firepower may expose Yi in the short term, but its protective plumage regrows fast.\r\n\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nA tamed Yi makes for an excellent -- if precarious -- hunting mount. Some tribes use them as caravan scouts, others to torment enemy forces.\r\nI’ve seen Yi scarf up the weird mushrooms, congealed gas balls, and nameless creatures scurrying around down here. Who knows what effect a diet like that would have on its feathery weaponry? Watch what your Yi eats, and you just might learn to sharpen its fighting skills!"

#. Key:	2873894402
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1255).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1255).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "GraphLiteral,2873894402"
msgid "Species: Cosmophasis luxtextor\r\nTime: Neogene\r\nDiet: Carnivore\r\nTemperament: Skittish\r\n\r\nWild\r\nI’ve found just the friend to get you through these low places, but you’re going to need to keep an open mind. Though I guess anyone that’s made it this far shouldn’t be afraid of an itsy bitsy jumping spider. Not to mention that there's more to this shy little charmer than meets the eyes (all eight of ‘em)…\r\nWith all the horrors crawling around down here, it’s easy to see why any spider might be a bit jumpy. And why it might have needed a clever new trick of its own to keep the shadows at bay.\r\nCosmophasis seems to know when predators can be chased away by its glowing webbing.That same danger-sense tells the spider to turn out the light when something comes seeking it.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nIt’s never easy to win someone over who’s had to hide their light away in dark times. But once you do, you’ll have a “ride or die” angel on your shoulder. Gain this spider’s trust, and Cosmophasis will help you out of sticky situations and put its life in your hands for the toughest fights ahead. And I mean that literally!\r\nYour fuzzy new friend can gobble up the webbing of larger spiders to recycle as ammunition. Then you can use it as a handheld web-shooter to slow down incoming enemies, snatch up items from the ground, and swing around like a spider-themed superhero! \r\n"
msgstr "Species: Cosmophasis luxtextor\r\nTime: Neogene\r\nDiet: Carnivore\r\nTemperament: Skittish\r\n\r\nWild\r\nI’ve found just the friend to get you through these low places, but you’re going to need to keep an open mind. Though I guess anyone that’s made it this far shouldn’t be afraid of an itsy bitsy jumping spider. Not to mention that there's more to this shy little charmer than meets the eyes (all eight of ‘em)…\r\nWith all the horrors crawling around down here, it’s easy to see why any spider might be a bit jumpy. And why it might have needed a clever new trick of its own to keep the shadows at bay.\r\nCosmophasis seems to know when predators can be chased away by its glowing webbing.That same danger-sense tells the spider to turn out the light when something comes seeking it.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nIt’s never easy to win someone over who’s had to hide their light away in dark times. But once you do, you’ll have a “ride or die” angel on your shoulder. Gain this spider’s trust, and Cosmophasis will help you out of sticky situations and put its life in your hands for the toughest fights ahead. And I mean that literally!\r\nYour fuzzy new friend can gobble up the webbing of larger spiders to recycle as ammunition. Then you can use it as a handheld web-shooter to slow down incoming enemies, snatch up items from the ground, and swing around like a spider-themed superhero! \r\n"

