#. Key:	4178095564
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(128).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(128).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "Content,4178095564"
msgid "Entry 7\nI never thought this island was normal, exactly. I mean, there are giant obelisks floating in the sky, for Pete's sake. Not to mention that cave I found, which hid a platform similar to those found at the base of said obelisks. Well, similar except for those oddly shaped holes that were carved into its podium.\n\nI guess I just didn't care about all that. So long as I had my beautiful, unique and untainted ecosystem to study, I was happy. But now…\n\nNo, I shouldn't write it off just yet. Not before I arrive at Rockwell's. There's still a chance that my data is off or that I missed something obvious. I won't give up on my paradise just yet."
msgstr "Entry 7\nI never thought this island was normal, exactly. I mean, there are giant obelisks floating in the sky, for Pete's sake. Not to mention that cave I found, which hid a platform similar to those found at the base of said obelisks. Well, similar except for those oddly shaped holes that were carved into its podium.\n\nI guess I just didn't care about all that. So long as I had my beautiful, unique and untainted ecosystem to study, I was happy. But now…\n\nNo, I shouldn't write it off just yet. Not before I arrive at Rockwell's. There's still a chance that my data is off or that I missed something obvious. I won't give up on my paradise just yet."

#. Key:	2976659729
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1282).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1282).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "GraphLiteral,2976659729"
msgid "Common Name: Armadoggo\r\nSpecies: Canis fodiensocius\r\nTime: Late Anthropocene\r\nDiet: Omnivore (but prefers meat)\r\nTemperament: Bestest!\r\n\r\nWild\r\nI’m told this clever canine was commonly sighted in areas contaminated by Element a few centuries after my time, so it may be one of the few lifeforms to have survived the collapse of the ecosphere. Like the dogs of my day, this is a social animal that hunts smaller prey in packs.\r\n\r\nThat bony, scaled shell likely protects Armadoggo from radiation while also allowing it to burrow through the ground. It can cross long distances tunneling under the surface to avoid larger threats. It doesn’t seem threatened by us, and is in fact unusually curious about humans.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nArmadoggo seems happier once tamed and eager to help, much like its pampered cousins from my time. And it’s one fiercely protective pet, alerting you to nearby dangers and even keeping some at bay.\r\n\r\nArmadoggo turns out to have a knack for tracking down valuable technological trinkets hoarded by some of the corrupted local wildlife. Once it picks up the scent from an Element-poisoned kill, it will take you to its hoarded, eminently weaponizable treasure.\r\n\r\nNo leash training needed -- your loyal new companion will happily carry gear and armaments while never straying too far away. If your Armadoggo gets hurt, it’s smart enough to hide away underground where it can lick its wounds until it can rejoin you. Looking around at the mess we made of the world, I’m not sure we deserve their devotion.\r\n"
msgstr "Common Name: Armadoggo\r\nSpecies: Canis fodiensocius\r\nTime: Late Anthropocene\r\nDiet: Omnivore (but prefers meat)\r\nTemperament: Bestest!\r\n\r\nWild\r\nI’m told this clever canine was commonly sighted in areas contaminated by Element a few centuries after my time, so it may be one of the few lifeforms to have survived the collapse of the ecosphere. Like the dogs of my day, this is a social animal that hunts smaller prey in packs.\r\n\r\nThat bony, scaled shell likely protects Armadoggo from radiation while also allowing it to burrow through the ground. It can cross long distances tunneling under the surface to avoid larger threats. It doesn’t seem threatened by us, and is in fact unusually curious about humans.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nArmadoggo seems happier once tamed and eager to help, much like its pampered cousins from my time. And it’s one fiercely protective pet, alerting you to nearby dangers and even keeping some at bay.\r\n\r\nArmadoggo turns out to have a knack for tracking down valuable technological trinkets hoarded by some of the corrupted local wildlife. Once it picks up the scent from an Element-poisoned kill, it will take you to its hoarded, eminently weaponizable treasure.\r\n\r\nNo leash training needed -- your loyal new companion will happily carry gear and armaments while never straying too far away. If your Armadoggo gets hurt, it’s smart enough to hide away underground where it can lick its wounds until it can rejoin you. Looking around at the mess we made of the world, I’m not sure we deserve their devotion.\r\n"

#. Key:	482619303
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1283).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1283).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "GraphLiteral,482619303"
msgid "Species: Dreadnoughtus recalcitrans\r\nTime: Late Cretaceous\r\nDiet: Herbivore\r\nTemperament: Territorial\r\n\r\nWild\r\nDreadnoughtus was a newly-described fossil genus in my day, named for the idea that it was so large it must’ve had nothing to fear in its own time. The current species does its namesake proud, and may even be able to defy the Titans ruling our post-apocalyptic world.\r\n\r\n\r\nA big believer in picking on creatures its own size, Dreadnoughtus will ignore anything smaller than a Spinosaur to focus on larger threats. It absorbs the fury of incoming attacks to charge up its uncanny, Element-disrupting bellow. Even then, this bruiser shows extraordinary patience, waiting until the last possible moment to come roaring back.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nOnce tamed, Dreadnoughtus will gladly shoulder your heaviest artillery and carry it -- and you -- into battle, on a platform big enough to earn that ‘dreadnought’ name. Your walking war-machine will swallow boulders (or cannonballs!) to spit back up as acid-coated ammo.\r\n\r\n\r\nIf you’re going to war with Titans, it just makes sense to recruit a titanic creature with a grudge against them. Your Dreadnoughtus ally may go down fighting, but not before softening up the adversary for a killing blow. Best of luck, and give ‘em Hell for me!\r\n"
msgstr "Species: Dreadnoughtus recalcitrans\r\nTime: Late Cretaceous\r\nDiet: Herbivore\r\nTemperament: Territorial\r\n\r\nWild\r\nDreadnoughtus was a newly-described fossil genus in my day, named for the idea that it was so large it must’ve had nothing to fear in its own time. The current species does its namesake proud, and may even be able to defy the Titans ruling our post-apocalyptic world.\r\n\r\n\r\nA big believer in picking on creatures its own size, Dreadnoughtus will ignore anything smaller than a Spinosaur to focus on larger threats. It absorbs the fury of incoming attacks to charge up its uncanny, Element-disrupting bellow. Even then, this bruiser shows extraordinary patience, waiting until the last possible moment to come roaring back.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nOnce tamed, Dreadnoughtus will gladly shoulder your heaviest artillery and carry it -- and you -- into battle, on a platform big enough to earn that ‘dreadnought’ name. Your walking war-machine will swallow boulders (or cannonballs!) to spit back up as acid-coated ammo.\r\n\r\n\r\nIf you’re going to war with Titans, it just makes sense to recruit a titanic creature with a grudge against them. Your Dreadnoughtus ally may go down fighting, but not before softening up the adversary for a killing blow. Best of luck, and give ‘em Hell for me!\r\n"

#. Key:	1731631716
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1284).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1284).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "GraphLiteral,1731631716"
msgid "Common Name: Deinotherium\r\nSpecies: Deinotherium Colossus\r\nTime: Late Miocene\r\nDiet: Herbivore\r\nTemperament: Territorial\r\n\r\nWild \r\nColossus felt like an appropriate name for such a massive variant of the already large elephant. Deinotherium dwarf their Mammoth cousins, and demonstrate terrifying strength and speed. You do not want to upset one of these! When angered, Deinotherium instead take an aggressive stance which results in a charge if the foe lingers for longer than welcome. In their hierachical based herds, aggrivated Deinotheriums will rally eachother with powerful roars. If you want to pick a fight with a herd, you should be ready.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nDeinotherium are no easy feat to tame. During the aggressive posture they exhibit, Deinotherium will show various signals to indicate whether they're bluffing in their attack or just want that tasty beer they smell! Once tamed, these terrible bests will handily tear down opposing structures and forces, as well as providing some powerful moral boosting calls!"
msgstr "Common Name: Deinotherium\r\nSpecies: Deinotherium Colossus\r\nTime: Late Miocene\r\nDiet: Herbivore\r\nTemperament: Territorial\r\n\r\nWild \r\nColossus felt like an appropriate name for such a massive variant of the already large elephant. Deinotherium dwarf their Mammoth cousins, and demonstrate terrifying strength and speed. You do not want to upset one of these! When angered, Deinotherium instead take an aggressive stance which results in a charge if the foe lingers for longer than welcome. In their hierachical based herds, aggrivated Deinotheriums will rally eachother with powerful roars. If you want to pick a fight with a herd, you should be ready.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nDeinotherium are no easy feat to tame. During the aggressive posture they exhibit, Deinotherium will show various signals to indicate whether they're bluffing in their attack or just want that tasty beer they smell! Once tamed, these terrible bests will handily tear down opposing structures and forces, as well as providing some powerful moral boosting calls!"

#. Key:	3937404796
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1285).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1285).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "GraphLiteral,3937404796"
msgid "Common Name: Cat\r\nSpecies: Felis catus\r\nTime: Holocene\r\nDiet: Carnivore\r\nTemperament: Cat\r\n\r\nWild\r\nWhy am I not surprised that common house cats survived the end of the world? Feral cats have always been reasonably good at avoiding wild predators… Maybe it’s more of a wonder they haven’t managed to wreck the ecosystem here, given their taste for the feathered dinosaur descendants of my day.\r\n\r\nCats are unpredictable little goblins. Some attack anyone who invades their personal space, some run and hide, and a few are actually friendly. But the really mean cats will cling onto you while trying to scratch your eyes out. You’ll need to slap those off your face while you still have one.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nThese fuzzy little fiends are fickle friends, but I’d bet you could win one over with a bit of meat or fish and some stroking of its malicious little ego. Not sure why you’d want to do that, exactly… Maybe they’d help keep thieving vermin from your camp? Or you could try drying out their spit-up hairballs for insulation…"
msgstr "Common Name: Cat\r\nSpecies: Felis catus\r\nTime: Holocene\r\nDiet: Carnivore\r\nTemperament: Cat\r\n\r\nWild\r\nWhy am I not surprised that common house cats survived the end of the world? Feral cats have always been reasonably good at avoiding wild predators… Maybe it’s more of a wonder they haven’t managed to wreck the ecosystem here, given their taste for the feathered dinosaur descendants of my day.\r\n\r\nCats are unpredictable little goblins. Some attack anyone who invades their personal space, some run and hide, and a few are actually friendly. But the really mean cats will cling onto you while trying to scratch your eyes out. You’ll need to slap those off your face while you still have one.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nThese fuzzy little fiends are fickle friends, but I’d bet you could win one over with a bit of meat or fish and some stroking of its malicious little ego. Not sure why you’d want to do that, exactly… Maybe they’d help keep thieving vermin from your camp? Or you could try drying out their spit-up hairballs for insulation…"

#. Key:	3773852531
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1286).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1286).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "GraphLiteral,3773852531"
msgid "Common Name: Bison\r\nSpecies: Bison matrislac\r\nTime: Early Pleistocene\r\nDiet: Herbivore\r\nTemperament: Territorial\r\n\r\nWild\r\nAfter dealing with the endless parade of mutants and freaks on the ARKs, it might feel like a pleasant surprise to run across some ordinary-looking buffalo! Just make sure to enjoy the local bison at a distance, if you’ve brought along anything as big as an allosaur -- they become aggressively territorial around larger predators.\r\n\r\nOnce provoked, you can expect these stubborn slabs of meat to fight until the bitter end. An agitated bison will throw that massive weight around to defend its herd even when overmatched. I watched one knock a rex senseless before tossing the dazed would-be predator off a cliff!\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nBreaking one of these overstuffed bovines is tougher than you might think... All that extra bulk makes bison especially tough to knock out. These loveable meatballs are worth taming, because they’ll fight for you as fiercely as their own herd. Your bison may not end fights quickly, but it will take a beating and tussle with just about any threat.\r\n\r\nA tame mama bison will prove to be a useful dairy animal for your tribe, as long as she’s nursing. That milk is extremely nutritious and actually pairs well with some of the more interesting recipes Survivors have concocted. Any nearby baby creature will want some, once they smell it… Heck, even baby wyverns treat it like their mother’s milk!"
msgstr "Common Name: Bison\r\nSpecies: Bison matrislac\r\nTime: Early Pleistocene\r\nDiet: Herbivore\r\nTemperament: Territorial\r\n\r\nWild\r\nAfter dealing with the endless parade of mutants and freaks on the ARKs, it might feel like a pleasant surprise to run across some ordinary-looking buffalo! Just make sure to enjoy the local bison at a distance, if you’ve brought along anything as big as an allosaur -- they become aggressively territorial around larger predators.\r\n\r\nOnce provoked, you can expect these stubborn slabs of meat to fight until the bitter end. An agitated bison will throw that massive weight around to defend its herd even when overmatched. I watched one knock a rex senseless before tossing the dazed would-be predator off a cliff!\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nBreaking one of these overstuffed bovines is tougher than you might think... All that extra bulk makes bison especially tough to knock out. These loveable meatballs are worth taming, because they’ll fight for you as fiercely as their own herd. Your bison may not end fights quickly, but it will take a beating and tussle with just about any threat.\r\n\r\nA tame mama bison will prove to be a useful dairy animal for your tribe, as long as she’s nursing. That milk is extremely nutritious and actually pairs well with some of the more interesting recipes Survivors have concocted. Any nearby baby creature will want some, once they smell it… Heck, even baby wyverns treat it like their mother’s milk!"

#. Key:	2689608362
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1287).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1287).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "GraphLiteral,2689608362"
msgid "Common Name: Drakelings\r\nSpecies: Draco temporanima\r\nTime: Unknown\r\nDiet: Carnivore (Prefers Cooked)\r\nTemperament: Curious\r\n\r\nWild\r\nI must be adapting to my weird new life on these ARKs, because I wasn’t even surprised to run across a new type of dragon. Despite its smaller stature, this friendly little fella seems more like the four-legged guardian of the Island than its bipedal wyvern cousins.\r\n\r\nSince all four varieties of Drakeling seem to be able to interbreed, I’ve decided they must all belong to the same species. Each type seems attuned to its own climate, making it tempting for survivors to sort them by the seasons we remember on Earth.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nDrakelings are drawn to our fires, and curious about people. Show one a bit of kindness and attention, and you’ll gain a powerful -- and portable -- new apprentice. These clever critters seem eager to learn, and to share their knowledge in turn. Let a Drakeling teach your other pets, and in return it will lead you to its hoarded treasure! \r\n\r\nThough all Drakelings can act like a reserve parachute, each seasonal variety also has its own powerful breath-attack and special gift. A spring Drakeling will invigorate you, while the summer variety will keep you moving. The autumnal breed empowers you and increases your harvest yields, while the wintery type insulates and cushions you from damage.\r\n"
msgstr "Common Name: Drakelings\r\nSpecies: Draco temporanima\r\nTime: Unknown\r\nDiet: Carnivore (Prefers Cooked)\r\nTemperament: Curious\r\n\r\nWild\r\nI must be adapting to my weird new life on these ARKs, because I wasn’t even surprised to run across a new type of dragon. Despite its smaller stature, this friendly little fella seems more like the four-legged guardian of the Island than its bipedal wyvern cousins.\r\n\r\nSince all four varieties of Drakeling seem to be able to interbreed, I’ve decided they must all belong to the same species. Each type seems attuned to its own climate, making it tempting for survivors to sort them by the seasons we remember on Earth.\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nDrakelings are drawn to our fires, and curious about people. Show one a bit of kindness and attention, and you’ll gain a powerful -- and portable -- new apprentice. These clever critters seem eager to learn, and to share their knowledge in turn. Let a Drakeling teach your other pets, and in return it will lead you to its hoarded treasure! \r\n\r\nThough all Drakelings can act like a reserve parachute, each seasonal variety also has its own powerful breath-attack and special gift. A spring Drakeling will invigorate you, while the summer variety will keep you moving. The autumnal breed empowers you and increases your harvest yields, while the wintery type insulates and cushions you from damage.\r\n"

#. Key:	872370676
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1288).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1288).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "GraphLiteral,872370676"
msgid "Common Name: Helicoprion\r\nSpecies: Helicoprion Frendo\r\nTime: Early Permian \r\nDiet: Soft bodied prey\r\nTemperament: Relaxed\r\n\r\nWild \r\nSimilar in size to Megaladon but far more calm, Helicoprion reclusively lurk in the open waters of the Arks ocean environments. While they are imposing with their familiar silhouette, Helicoprion pose no threat unless provvoked. That is, unless you happen to be an unfortunate ammonite, which Helicoprion have a strong desire to chomp down on!\r\n\r\nWith strong jaws and an evven stronger tail, Helicoprion can pose a dangerous threat if they'e been bothered. The jaws of Helicoprion are used to quickly and efficiently slice up the soft bodies of cephalopod creatures, and the tail in particular has evolvved to be more like a Thresher sharks. This significant change is used to great effect at stunning prey. Some survviving claim more powerful Heli can evven knock out foes with a powerful whip of their tail!\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nThe truly remarkable jaw of Helicoprion can be used for much more than just eating Ammonites. Oddly enough, the precise cutting power the tooth whorl provvides can be used to seperate the various components of equipment. For some older specimens, this process allows survivors to record the crafting requirements for their various equipment. I've only seen the most experienced crafters employ this bizarre utility, but it's an astonishingly useful one for sure!"
msgstr "Common Name: Helicoprion\r\nSpecies: Helicoprion Frendo\r\nTime: Early Permian \r\nDiet: Soft bodied prey\r\nTemperament: Relaxed\r\n\r\nWild \r\nSimilar in size to Megaladon but far more calm, Helicoprion reclusively lurk in the open waters of the Arks ocean environments. While they are imposing with their familiar silhouette, Helicoprion pose no threat unless provvoked. That is, unless you happen to be an unfortunate ammonite, which Helicoprion have a strong desire to chomp down on!\r\n\r\nWith strong jaws and an evven stronger tail, Helicoprion can pose a dangerous threat if they'e been bothered. The jaws of Helicoprion are used to quickly and efficiently slice up the soft bodies of cephalopod creatures, and the tail in particular has evolvved to be more like a Thresher sharks. This significant change is used to great effect at stunning prey. Some survviving claim more powerful Heli can evven knock out foes with a powerful whip of their tail!\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nThe truly remarkable jaw of Helicoprion can be used for much more than just eating Ammonites. Oddly enough, the precise cutting power the tooth whorl provvides can be used to seperate the various components of equipment. For some older specimens, this process allows survivors to record the crafting requirements for their various equipment. I've only seen the most experienced crafters employ this bizarre utility, but it's an astonishingly useful one for sure!"

#. Key:	2550034649
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1289).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1289).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "GraphLiteral,2550034649"
msgid "Common Name: Megaraptor\r\nSpecies: Megaraptor Sceleratus\r\nTime: Late Cretaceous\r\nDiet: Carnivore\r\nTemperament: Solitary Aggressive\r\n\r\nWild\r\nTrust me, you don't want one of these creepy killers hunting you. Megaraptor picks off its civtims one at a time, like the villain in an old-school slasher flick. And once you've been targeted, your stalker will scare away your friends and allies with its unnerving glare.\r\n\r\nYou won't see this monster coming until it's right on top of you. That's when megaraptor pounces, pinning you down and scattering everyone nearby to leave you defenseless. Talk about a jump scare! The final indignity is that your death will give the beast the strength to shrug off any attempt to avenge you. \r\n\r\nDomesticatedBefore you can tame a Megaraptor, you'll have to earn its respect. Find and claim its grisly altar of carcasses, then keep the enraged owner at bay with fire. Just make sure you stay on a constant swivel, because this fiend will fight like hell for its trophies.\r\n\r\nManage to break one of these butchers, and you'll share all the meat from its many kills. You'll also gain an assassin eager to isolate and murder your enemies. But know that Megaraptor hunts alone. Uncomfotable around even its own kind, this army of one will lose its fighting edge around others."
msgstr "Common Name: Megaraptor\r\nSpecies: Megaraptor Sceleratus\r\nTime: Late Cretaceous\r\nDiet: Carnivore\r\nTemperament: Solitary Aggressive\r\n\r\nWild\r\nTrust me, you don't want one of these creepy killers hunting you. Megaraptor picks off its civtims one at a time, like the villain in an old-school slasher flick. And once you've been targeted, your stalker will scare away your friends and allies with its unnerving glare.\r\n\r\nYou won't see this monster coming until it's right on top of you. That's when megaraptor pounces, pinning you down and scattering everyone nearby to leave you defenseless. Talk about a jump scare! The final indignity is that your death will give the beast the strength to shrug off any attempt to avenge you. \r\n\r\nDomesticatedBefore you can tame a Megaraptor, you'll have to earn its respect. Find and claim its grisly altar of carcasses, then keep the enraged owner at bay with fire. Just make sure you stay on a constant swivel, because this fiend will fight like hell for its trophies.\r\n\r\nManage to break one of these butchers, and you'll share all the meat from its many kills. You'll also gain an assassin eager to isolate and murder your enemies. But know that Megaraptor hunts alone. Uncomfotable around even its own kind, this army of one will lose its fighting edge around others."

#. Key:	2126901582
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(129).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(129).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "Content,2126901582"
msgid "Entry 6\nAs absurd as the Death Worms are, I see their purpose. They are a natural barrier. \n\nIf this whole place is a space station posing as a real ecosystem, then it needs to keep the wildlife and humans inside it contained in a way that appears natural. The island accomplished that by simply being an island, and the desert does it via the Death Worms.\n\nGranted, a large population of predators with ludicrous kilocalorie needs would never last long in a place devoid of prey, but as this is an artificial environment, the station can feed or replace the Death Worms as needed. It's a little convoluted, but it makes sense."
msgstr "Entry 6\nAs absurd as the Death Worms are, I see their purpose. They are a natural barrier. \n\nIf this whole place is a space station posing as a real ecosystem, then it needs to keep the wildlife and humans inside it contained in a way that appears natural. The island accomplished that by simply being an island, and the desert does it via the Death Worms.\n\nGranted, a large population of predators with ludicrous kilocalorie needs would never last long in a place devoid of prey, but as this is an artificial environment, the station can feed or replace the Death Worms as needed. It's a little convoluted, but it makes sense."

#. Key:	440971890
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1290).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
#: /Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1290).ExplorerNoteEntries.LocalizedSubtitle
msgctxt "GraphLiteral,440971890"
msgid "Species: Osto silvanimus\r\nTime: Unknown\r\nDiet: Carnivore\r\nTemperament: Passive\r\n\r\nWild\r\nThis time it feels like I’m trying to describe a supernatural entity, instead of the usual living fossil. Crossing paths with Osto Silvanimus (commonly called Elderclaw) is like communing with a forest’s soul. As much flora as fauna, this ursine avatar seems attuned to local plant life.\r\n\r\nAlthough Elderclaw rarely attacks unprovoked, it won’t shy away from a fight. It instinctively controls nearby vegetation to wrap itself in a thick tangle of protective vines, while sending out roots to entangle any aggressor... assuming it doesn't just knock them into a stupor with its massive paws.\r\n\r\nA nettled Elderclaw will conjure up a Spirit Echo to help ward off threats. This ethereal talisman will then emanate vibrations that weaken threats while invigorating those in its protection. A Spirit Echo collects the energies of nearby violence and imbues its Elderclaw master with enough fury and power to trigger explosive growth.\r\n\r\nWhile Spirit Echoes generally stay immaterial, they can also become tangible long enough to explode. The resulting cloud of charged spores will affect friend and foe differently, burning aggressors but closing wounds on allies.\r\n\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nWhile I've yet to see a tame adult Elderclaw, their cubs (or shrubs?) seem much more impressionable. Sadly, this has led Survivors to hunt and kill these lumbering giants for their seeds. Propagating your own is a long, difficult process, but once a sproutling has grown enough to leave its garden, you’ll gain a protective forest warden that can use its roots to sustainably harvest nearby trees.\r\n\r\n"
msgstr "Species: Osto silvanimus\r\nTime: Unknown\r\nDiet: Carnivore\r\nTemperament: Passive\r\n\r\nWild\r\nThis time it feels like I’m trying to describe a supernatural entity, instead of the usual living fossil. Crossing paths with Osto Silvanimus (commonly called Elderclaw) is like communing with a forest’s soul. As much flora as fauna, this ursine avatar seems attuned to local plant life.\r\n\r\nAlthough Elderclaw rarely attacks unprovoked, it won’t shy away from a fight. It instinctively controls nearby vegetation to wrap itself in a thick tangle of protective vines, while sending out roots to entangle any aggressor... assuming it doesn't just knock them into a stupor with its massive paws.\r\n\r\nA nettled Elderclaw will conjure up a Spirit Echo to help ward off threats. This ethereal talisman will then emanate vibrations that weaken threats while invigorating those in its protection. A Spirit Echo collects the energies of nearby violence and imbues its Elderclaw master with enough fury and power to trigger explosive growth.\r\n\r\nWhile Spirit Echoes generally stay immaterial, they can also become tangible long enough to explode. The resulting cloud of charged spores will affect friend and foe differently, burning aggressors but closing wounds on allies.\r\n\r\n\r\nDomesticated\r\nWhile I've yet to see a tame adult Elderclaw, their cubs (or shrubs?) seem much more impressionable. Sadly, this has led Survivors to hunt and kill these lumbering giants for their seeds. Propagating your own is a long, difficult process, but once a sproutling has grown enough to leave its garden, you’ll gain a protective forest warden that can use its roots to sustainably harvest nearby trees.\r\n\r\n"

#. Key:	1307510119
#. SourceLocation:	/Game/PrimalEarth/CoreBlueprints/PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP.Default__PrimalGameDataARKNX_BP_C.ExplorerNoteEntries(1291).ExplorerNoteEntries.BinkMediaSubtitles(0).BinkMediaSubtitles.BaseText
